[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 4(Title: Out of the Past) In Germany she married (1892) a successful actor, Emanuel Beck, who soon abandoned her, whereupon she took (1893) the name Isak Dinesen; in 1906 she married the Danish nobleman Johann Klug Christiansen (Dinesen, 1868–1940). In 1931, seeking solitude, she bought Den Falk (Swan Island), near Nyborg, on the isle of Funen; and from 1940 to 1945 she operated a Danish coffee shop and restaurant in Nairobi, Kenya, with a Danish friend. After 1945 she lived primarily in England, and then Kenya (1949–68). After years of refusing publication, she became one of the most celebrated novelists of her time, winning international acclaim with Seven Gothic Tales (1934). In 1937 she published Ali and Nino, a romantic novel that was lost during World War II but was rediscovered in Russian. All of Dinesen's stories
