[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 5(Title: Saint-Laurent ) Saint-Laurent Saint-Laurent (English: St-Laurent, also the homonym Île Saint-Laurent) is an island in the St. Lawrence River. It is located upstream from the mouth of the Saguenay River. An artificial causeway links the island to the mainland of the Saguenay Lowland on the north shore of the river. The island can be accessed by ferry from the peninsula of Pointe-des-Monts, whose airport is named after the island. The island is densely covered by the only boreal forest on the coast of the river, north of the mouth of the Saguenay River. The forest mainly consists of black spruce and tamarack. Due to the extreme continental climate, the timber is highly resistant to decay. The island is protected as a nature reserve since 1966.
