[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Goethe, Johann Wolfgang von Goethe, was a prominent German writer, philosopher, scientist, and politician during the late 18th and early 19th centuries. He was born on August 28, 1749, in Frankfurt am Main and passed away on March 22, 1832, in Weimar. Goethe is considered one of the most significant literary figures in German history, known for his works such as "Faust," "The Sorrows of Young Werther," and "Wilhelm Meister's Apprenticeship." His influence extended beyond literature, as he also contributed to science, particularly through his studies of botany and optics. In recognition of his contributions, there are numerous Goethe institutes and monuments worldwide.
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Rodin, however, is not directly related to Goethe and is actually a French sculptor and artist, active in the late 19th and early 20th centuries. His full name was François Auguste René Rodin, born November 12, 1840, in Paris, and he died on November 17, 1917, also in Paris. He is best known for his sculptures, "The Thinker" and "The Kiss," among many others. Rodin's innovative techniques and exploration of human emotions through sculpture had a profound impact on the art world, and he is often considered one of the most influential sculptors in history.
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m To summarize, Johann Wolfgang von Goethe was a German writer and polymath who lived in the late 18th and early 19th centuries and significantly contributed to various fields including literature and science. On the other hand, François Auguste René Rodin was a French sculptor and artist active in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, renowned for his sculptures and his contributions to the art world.
