[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 1(Title: "Electron transport chain") many as three proton pumps, like mitochondria, or they may contain only one or two. They always contain at least one proton pump. In the present day biosphere, the most common electron donors are organic molecules. Organisms that use organic molecules as an electron source are called ""organotrophs"". Organotrophs (animals, fungi, protists) and ""phototrophs"" (plants and algae) constitute the vast majority of all familiar life forms. Some prokaryotes can use inorganic matter as an energy source. Such an organism is called a ""lithotroph"" (""rock-eater""). Inorganic electron donors include hydrogen, carbon monoxide, ammonia, nitrite, sulfur, sulfide, manganese oxide, and ferrous iron.
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 2(Title: Cyanobacteria) continuous with the plasma membrane but are separate compartments. The photosynthetic machinery is embedded in the thylakoid membranes, with phycobilisomes acting as light-harvesting antennae attached to the membrane, giving the green pigmentation observed (with wavelengths from 450 nm to 660 nm) in most cyanobacteria. While most of the high-energy electrons derived from water are used by the cyanobacterial cells for their own needs, a fraction of these electrons may be donated to the external environment via electrogenic activity. Respiration in cyanobacteria can occur in the thylakoid membrane alongside photosynthesis, with their photosynthetic electron transport sharing the same compartment as the
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 3(Title: "Green sulfur bacteria") PS I for photosynthesis. Thousands of bacteriochlorophyll(BCHl) c, d and e of the cells absorb light at 720-750 nm, and the light energy is transferred to BChl a-795 and a-808 before being transferred to Fenna-Matthews-Olson (FMO)-proteins which are connected to reaction centers (RC). The FMO complex then transfers the excitation energy to the RC with its special pair which absorbs at 840 nm in the plasma membrane. After the reaction centers receive the energy, electrons are ejected and transferred through electron transport chains (ETCs). Some electrons form Fe-S clusters; others are transferred to quinone and then to PQ. The electrons finally transfer to the Rieske iron-sulfur protein. So, in essence, the Rieske iron-sulfur protein acts as a H+ pump, taking protons from the outside to the inside of the plasma membrane. While the photosynthetic electron transport occurs in the plasma membrane, respiration takes place in the cytoplasm and on the plasma membrane. 
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 4(Title: "Photosystem I") accept the majority of the excitation energy and give rise to electron transport via the PS II to the plastoquinone pool (PQ). The standard photophosphorylation reaction starts when the electrons reach the electron transport system in the PQ pool, which transfers the electrons to a cytochrome b6f complex (similar to the mitochondria ATP synthase). The cytochrome b6f pump is located at the thylakoid membrane and is a heterotrimeric protein complex that contains at least seven different polypeptides. Two of its subunits contain plant-specific chlorophylls and bacteriochlorophylls, collectively known as cloroxanthins. Oxygenic PSII oxidizes H2O to release oxygen, protons, and electrons. The electrons are carried by a chain of carriers to a special protein that transfers them to a newly synthesized ATP. The protons produced as a consequence of the photolysis of H2O and the subsequent proton movements across the thylakoid membrane create a proton motive force, which accounts for 75% of the ATP formation in the chloroplasts. 
[36m(main_task pid=77121)[0m Doc 5(Title: "Photosystem II") light energy (which is produced through photosystem I) is transferred to the protein complex and it further transferred to quinone (Q) electron carrier. The electrons picked up from water by photosystem II are transferred through the light-independent electron transport chain to the fourth terminal electron carrier, plastocyanin (Pc). In thylakoid membranes, they are sequentially transferred to different electron acceptor molecules. The most significant acceptor molecules include: plastoquinone, PQ; plastocyanin, PC; cytochrome b6/f complex, b6f; ferredoxin, Fd; and NADPH. 
